 transfer learning, so in this case we will look into different technical aspects of adaptation,

3
00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:39,440
 the objective would be to take the source model, pretend model and we try to see how

4
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:45,640
 we can adopt this particular model to the target task that we have, so we will see different

5
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:53,000
 sorts of challenges and then how we can like you know use some methodologies to tackle

6
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,600
 some of these problems and so on so forth, so the question is how to adapt, you know

7
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:08,400
 it is all about taking one particular model, applying to a new task, so there are several

8
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:14,260
 things that we have to keep in mind, first of all the architectural modifications, you

9
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:23,380
 know now again stepping back for a while, what we are trying to achieve in this case,

10
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:34,060
 we have a pre-trained model, existing model and we would like to make some changes to

11
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:42,840
 the model for a new task without retraining for this particular task from scratch, because

12
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:48,960
 we have limited data for the target task, we might have computational burden, computational

13
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:55,140
 limitations and so on so forth, so the question is how much we should change to the pre-trained

14
00:01:55,140 --> 00:02:01,160
 model architectures for adaptation and there are various degrees of changes, change only

15
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:04,760
 one layer, two layer, three layer, all sorts of things and we will see different kinds

16
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:11,040
 of modifications for this adaptation.

17
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:16,080
 Second thing is optimization schemes, now it is not only that you know the architectural

18
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:23,480
 modification will give you all the necessary things, you need to also look into the optimization

19
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:29,800
 schemes to deal with this little bit of extra training that you need in order to adapt to

20
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:36,400
 the new task, so which ways to train during adaptation, what schedule you should follow,

21
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:42,600
 what should set the learning rate and all sorts of things and third, can we use more

22
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:49,000
 signals for example, the multitasking architectures, can we use signals from multiple tasks simultaneously

23
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,360
 or you can ensemble things from different places and so on so forth, so these are some

24
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:59,640
 of these things that we basically need to look into when we are trying to do adaptation.

25
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:06,480
 Now as a programmer and as a designer, the adaptation is something extremely critical

26
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:13,320
 for the deep learning, the pre-training and deep learning models.

27
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:22,280
 So let us look into the architectural changes, what we can do because often and almost always

28
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:28,320
 we have to do the architectural changes because we take one existing model, we make some changes

29
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:31,240
 and we apply to a new task.

30
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:38,520
 So broadly we achieve these things in two ways, number one we keep the pre-trained model

31
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:46,240
 internals unchanged, we do not even touch upon the internals, what we do, we do a very

32
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:50,920
 shallow thing like we add some classifier on top for example, if your objective is to

33
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:56,200
 do image classification and you already have say pre-trained image vectors, then you simply

34
00:03:56,200 --> 00:04:04,540
 add one classification layer on top like polyconnected layer, softmax layer and so on so forth.

35
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:09,840
 So you just add classifier on the top embedding at the bottom, use outputs as features and

36
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:15,120
 so on so forth, so without changing the internal structure of this model, the model remains

37
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:21,120
 same as is only few changes here and there.

38
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:26,960
 The second thing is basically modifying the pre-trained model internal architecture and

39
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:33,600
 that is something little bit more intricate than the previous one and like the situation

40
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:39,160
 where this thing needs to be done is basically that if the task that we are dealing with

41
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:44,320
 is very different from what we already had, this whole task.

42
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:48,400
 So in a situation like this we may need to like initialize the encoder decoder slightly

43
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:53,400
 different way, we may need to play with the encoder architectures and so on so forth.

44
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:56,640
 We may need to add some adapters, special kind of things and so on and we will see in

45
00:04:56,640 --> 00:04:59,240
 a moment how you can do all of this thing.

46
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:06,760
 So this is the first example of keeping your model architecture unchanged almost.

47
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:13,400
 You just do little bit of very shallow processing on top of the existing model.

48
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:18,600
 So what we do in this case, we simply remove the pre-training task head.

49
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:22,800
 So if the architect is something like this, this is for example the original architecture,

50
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:30,640
 so what we do is simply go and remove this particular one and we simply add for example

51
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:34,280
 as one more layer something like this here.

52
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:39,560
 So if there are four classes, we simply go and add and this is a fully connected layer

53
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:43,640
 or maybe it could be a softmax layer something like this.

54
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:50,480
 So you take out topmost layer, assuming that this particular task is for say image classification

55
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:53,440
 and you have to do some kind of image segmentation or something like that.

56
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:57,760
 We take out the layer for image classification, we add one more layer suitable for image segmentation

57
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:01,560
 and simply get things done.

58
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:06,840
 So in language model for example what we do, we basically add a particular layer depending

59
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:11,000
 upon the task that we are dealing with whether this is a process of tagging, whether classification

60
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,960
 and other things and so on and so forth.

61
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:17,880
 In a sense that we don't touch upon the other layers only we remove the top one and then

62
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:19,880
 make the changes.

63
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:26,640
 And this is something actually pretty common, comparatively very light weight and very easy

64
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:32,280
 to do in programming as well.

65
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:38,760
 Here we can add target specific layers on top or the bottom of the piton layer is also

66
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:43,780
 you can be added in the top layer or the bottom layer specific to the particular target.

67
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:48,160
 Again we are not going to change the internals of this particular architecture.

68
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:52,520
 The architecture remains the same, only the top or the bottom layers can be changed depending

69
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:56,960
 upon the task that we are dealing with.

70
00:06:56,960 --> 00:07:03,400
 So often what we add, we add basically a single linear layer on top of the piton model and

71
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:09,320
 again retrain this particular model with the little bit of data that we have for the target

72
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:14,320
 task.

73
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:19,800
 So another one is basically more complex tasks for example we have to do, we can use the separate

74
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:24,400
 models for input, for output and so on and so forth.

75
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:30,640
 And this kind of architecture is extremely useful when we have to basically do the interaction

76
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:37,480
 between different tasks and the embeddings are not available beforehand and so on and

77
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:38,480
 so forth.

78
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:39,480
 So something like this.

79
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:43,920
 So in a situation like this what we can do, we can basically use the multiple inputs coming

80
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:50,480
 from different possible signals and basically we can concatenate all of the signals to a

81
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:55,160
 single vector and simply pass it along to the corresponding architectures.

82
00:07:55,160 --> 00:08:00,480
 Once again we are not changing these architectures in any way, we are simply using these input

83
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:05,520
 signals or changing this output layer just to adapt to the corresponding target task

84
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:10,400
 that we are dealing with.

85
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:15,400
 Now the more challenging thing is basically modifying the model internals.

86
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:21,300
 In the previous case very little changes like either you change little bit in the input layer

87
00:08:21,300 --> 00:08:26,520
 or you change little bit of the output layer and so on so forth.

88
00:08:26,520 --> 00:08:31,120
 So in this case we would like to play with the changes in the model internals.

89
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:39,040
 The structure will go through numerous changes and we have to do little bit more processing.

90
00:08:39,040 --> 00:08:45,960
 So this is required when the target task is structurally very different from the source

91
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:52,720
 task because we have taken the model from the source task to different trying to apply

92
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:58,160
 to a target task so if they are structurally very different the same architecture might

93
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:00,480
 not work very well.

94
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:08,160
 So in a situation like this we have to change the structure of this model some internals.

95
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:16,120
 So, for example, you pretend to the single input sequence but you are trying to adapt

96
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:19,760
 to a task with several input sequences for example.

97
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:25,440
 Say your objective was sentence classification, you are applying this for next sentence prediction

98
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:29,680
 tasks.

99
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:33,520
 So typical approach that we follow in this case, we pretend the model's weight to initialize

100
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:36,600
 as much as possible of a structurally different target task model.

101
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:41,320
 So whatever weight we had in the original model we will use that and then basically on top

102
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:51,920
 of this we are going to retrain this model based on the target data that we have.

103
00:09:51,920 --> 00:09:56,320
 So often what we do is basically use following your language models to initialize encoder

104
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:59,040
 and decoder parameters for machine translation.

105
00:09:59,040 --> 00:10:06,840
 So if our objective is to do machine translation for example, we can basically learn or basically

106
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:10,880
 lend the weights from the encoders and decoders and then straightaway apply for the machine

107
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:16,600
 translation task.

108
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:19,640
 So this is something that basically we could do here and there can change a lot of changes

109
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:24,000
 in this model architectures.

110
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:33,280
 Now for task specific modifications, we need to basically do, we need to add some capabilities.

111
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:39,200
 So we provide the pretend models with capabilities and this such that they are useful for the

112
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:42,680
 target tasks.

113
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:48,080
 So one of the common thing for example we do in say that language processing or in vision

114
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:54,240
 models and so on and so forth adding skip connections, residual connections or attentions

115
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:56,920
 and so on and so forth.

116
00:10:56,920 --> 00:11:01,960
 So in the simpler case when we are not modifying these architectures, we are not adding anything

117
00:11:01,960 --> 00:11:02,960
 like that.

118
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:06,880
 We are simply adding one more extra layer on top of it say fully connected layer, soft

119
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:08,720
 back layer and so on and so forth.

120
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:12,800
 No skip connection, no residual connection, no attention, nothing like that.

121
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:18,440
 But in this case because the target task is more demanding, structurally different than

122
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:25,520
 the source task, we are going to add say for example NLP, the attention layers or say in

123
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:34,960
 image we can use the residual connections like in ResNet and so on and so forth.

124
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:42,160
 So one of the major goals in adaptation that we must use the least number of parameters

125
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:48,920
 because the amount of data that we have is lesser compared to what we should have and

126
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:53,560
 therefore the number of parameter that we should use in a situation like this should

127
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:58,960
 be lesser than the original model.

128
00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:04,040
 So we basically have to do less parameter tuning because this is anyway going to be fine tuning

129
00:12:04,040 --> 00:12:06,560
 on sitting on top of this original model.

130
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:14,960
 So it is very useful given the increasing size of this model parameters and we generally

131
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:19,780
 add the bottleneck modules to achieve this called adapters.

132
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:25,120
 Adapter is typically a connecting component between different layers of the pretend models.

133
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:30,160
 So one of these adapters could be basically additional attention layer or it could be

134
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:35,800
 say for example the skip connection between two layers and so on and so forth.

135
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:40,320
 So this picture is something like this that there are different kinds of layers.

136
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:46,080
 So the A1 adapter is basically connecting this particular layer if I number this in

137
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:51,400
 this way 3 and 4.

138
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:59,880
 So this A1 is basically connecting 1 to 2, specific kind of adapter which will fulfill

139
00:12:59,880 --> 00:13:04,400
 to some extent of course cannot fully fulfill the demand that we are having for the target

140
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:10,040
 task to some extent can fulfill the requirement and the limitations of this pretend model.

141
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:13,520
 Then A2 is connecting these two layers adapter.

142
00:13:13,520 --> 00:13:16,640
 This is connecting these two layers and adapter and so on so forth.

143
00:13:16,640 --> 00:13:21,800
 So as I mentioned the adapters could be something like the residual connections or the attentions

144
00:13:21,800 --> 00:13:24,000
 and so on so forth.

145
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:33,200
 So the broad objective of the adapters basically is to fine tune this features little more given

146
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:34,920
 the nature of the task that we are dealing with.

147
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:45,680
 If the task is complex we require more fine tuning and therefore we have to be very careful.

148
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:51,240
 So as I mentioned the common thing that we do is residual connections in parallel to

149
00:13:51,240 --> 00:14:00,240
 an existing layer so we can add residual connections between two different layers and so on.

150
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:07,640
 So often we do like we basically interleave these adapters in between different layers

151
00:14:07,640 --> 00:14:15,240
 so that they can learn better representations from different layers.

152
00:14:15,240 --> 00:14:20,840
 And so this could be different for example when this is a vision task we could add the

153
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:23,520
 convolutional kind of things.

154
00:14:23,520 --> 00:14:27,680
 When you are dealing with NLP tasks we can add the self attention layers and so on so

155
00:14:27,680 --> 00:14:32,620
 forth because the goals are different and therefore the adapters that we basically play

156
00:14:32,620 --> 00:14:36,800
 with are different.

157
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:43,440
 So this is one good reference where they have used adapters to adapt these new models to

158
00:14:43,440 --> 00:14:49,760
 the new tasks and achieved good performance even with the limited amount of data on the

159
00:14:49,760 --> 00:14:54,640
 target domain.

160
00:14:54,640 --> 00:14:59,920
 Next important component is weight update schedule.

161
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:04,480
 Now let us step back for a moment and try to think about on which set of we are standing

162
00:15:04,480 --> 00:15:06,560
 so far.

163
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:13,320
 So we have original model, pretend model, we made some changes here and there maybe

164
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:18,880
 the simple changes like at top or at bottom or maybe like the model internals and so on

165
00:15:18,880 --> 00:15:21,280
 so forth.

166
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:25,600
 So as you know a typical deep learning model is nothing but a collection of parameters

167
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:28,160
 that means the weights and biases.

168
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:34,280
 So the moment you make any changes to the model we have to learn this new weights accordingly

169
00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:37,640
 as nicely as possible.

170
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:42,640
 So because somewhere we are making some changes, somewhere things are constant.

171
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:48,200
 So what strategies we should take to update the weights and that is a very critical thing

172
00:15:48,200 --> 00:15:53,360
 in deep learning pre-training as deep learning like the domain adaptation or this transfer

173
00:15:53,360 --> 00:15:57,480
 learning and deep learning.

174
00:15:57,480 --> 00:16:04,200
 So the question is we have to update weights for sure but in which order this is something

175
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:08,480
 extremely critical and how should we do that?

176
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:11,320
 There could be many strategies to do that.

177
00:16:11,320 --> 00:16:17,160
 So in which order we should do it and how should we do that?

178
00:16:17,160 --> 00:16:27,920
 But the main goal that we have to keep in mind is that that in doing so we must not

179
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:34,800
 overwrite the information already there in the pre-trained models because the pre-trained

180
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:42,240
 models giving us the necessary background and the backbone on which we will be standing.

181
00:16:42,240 --> 00:16:49,480
 Now if by adding new things we are going to change the weights to a great deal then the

182
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:53,480
 models are going to be changed a lot.

183
00:16:53,480 --> 00:17:01,480
 So the original information will be lost and therefore this seriously questions the capabilities

184
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:06,320
 of this model, generalizations of this model on the new data and so on and so forth.

185
00:17:06,320 --> 00:17:10,000
 So while for sure we have to change the weights we have to keep in mind that we are not going

186
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:15,800
 to overwrite the existing information.

187
00:17:15,800 --> 00:17:20,360
 So common approach for weight update is basically from top to bottom.

188
00:17:20,360 --> 00:17:30,960
 So you have multiple layers for example 1, 2, 3, 4 and every layer have some weights.

189
00:17:30,960 --> 00:17:38,600
 So I consider this w1, this is w2, w3 and so on so forth.

190
00:17:38,600 --> 00:17:44,240
 So the common approach that we follow very cost effective, easy to implement and all

191
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:50,400
 sorts of things is basically update from top to bottom.

192
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:56,560
 So you progressively in time we simply freeze the other layers, the weights in the other

193
00:17:56,560 --> 00:18:04,280
 layers and simply update a particular layer one at a time from top to bottom that is very

194
00:18:04,280 --> 00:18:05,280
 common.

195
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:19,180
 For example when I am trying to change this particular weight w3, w2 and w1 they will

196
00:18:19,180 --> 00:18:20,180
 remain constant.

197
00:18:20,180 --> 00:18:23,560
 I am not going to do anything on this.

198
00:18:23,560 --> 00:18:27,920
 Then once I am done with w3 I achieved a good performance based on the data that I have

199
00:18:27,920 --> 00:18:33,960
 in the target domain then I will basically freeze this one then I will go to the w2,

200
00:18:33,960 --> 00:18:41,880
 make some changes while keeping other things frozen and then simply proceed.

201
00:18:41,880 --> 00:18:46,720
 The other thing is basically progressively in intensity.

202
00:18:46,720 --> 00:18:53,320
 So the learning rate is something is extremely critical for good optimization for getting

203
00:18:53,320 --> 00:19:00,080
 the right objective for speeding up this model and so on so forth.

204
00:19:00,080 --> 00:19:05,520
 Now the question is how we should vary the learning rates?

205
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:09,960
 What should the schedule for varying the learning rates?

206
00:19:09,960 --> 00:19:15,840
 And then progressively versus the pre-tent model the regularization stuff.

207
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:20,040
 How we can basically make the model little regularized so that we do not lose any information

208
00:19:20,040 --> 00:19:22,160
 that we already had.

209
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:29,280
 So in weight freezing what we do and that is the main intuition which drives the weight

210
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:34,640
 freezing is that we train all layers on data with different distribution and tasks like

211
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:37,840
 if we try to do that may lead to instability and poor solutions.

212
00:19:37,840 --> 00:19:43,400
 There is a main concern that we have that we simultaneously try to learn all the layers

213
00:19:43,400 --> 00:19:48,520
 and so on so forth may dismantle the entire thing that we have learnt because the data

214
00:19:48,520 --> 00:19:54,040
 that we are dealing with very small in quantity and we cannot do very much.

215
00:19:54,040 --> 00:19:59,680
 So then why not take this particular approach where we are not going to dismantle everything

216
00:19:59,680 --> 00:20:00,680
 simultaneously.

217
00:20:00,680 --> 00:20:06,600
 We simply take one at a time, make the changes, freeze it and then take the others.

218
00:20:06,600 --> 00:20:11,680
 That is the more or less this idea.

219
00:20:11,680 --> 00:20:15,800
 So we take individual layers, we adapt it to the new task, see how they are performing

220
00:20:15,800 --> 00:20:22,000
 on the new task and simply freeze it and then proceed.

221
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:26,920
 So this something is called the layer wise training which is very old technique invented

222
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:33,440
 by one of the pioneers of deep learning back in 2006 and that is still widely used in the

223
00:20:33,440 --> 00:20:40,120
 transfer learning based on the deep learning models.

224
00:20:40,120 --> 00:20:45,120
 There are other approaches, very common approaches like chain thaw, gradual unfreezing, sequential

